<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duke Wu of Qin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 678 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duke Wu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> from 697 to 678 BCE. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>, in 688 BCE he turned conquered territory at Gui and Ji into counties (xian) governed directly by the ruler, among the earliest known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Shiji also records that 66 people were buried with him, the first recorded case of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> at a Qin ruler's funeral.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Duke Wu, whose personal name is unknown, was the eldest son and crown prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-xian-of-qin">Duke Xian of Qin</a>. When his father died in 704 BCE at the age of 21, the ministers Fuji and Sanfu set him aside and placed his younger half-brother Chuzi on the throne. Six years later, in 698 BCE, the same ministers murdered Chuzi and installed Duke Wu, the original crown prince, in his place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 697 BCE, the first year of his reign, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> attacked the Pengxi tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong people</a>, and its army advanced east to Mount Hua. In 695 BCE Duke Wu had Sanfu, Fuji and their clans executed for the murder of Chuzi. The counties he set up at Gui and Ji in 688 BCE lay in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianshui">Tianshui</a> and Gangu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>. The following year he established the counties of Du and Zheng in the east, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-county">Hua County</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, and conquered the minor state of Xiao Guo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Duke Wu died in 678 BCE after a reign of 20 years and was buried at Yong, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengxiang">Fengxiang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. Although he had a son named Bai, he was succeeded by his younger brother, Duke De of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, who moved the capital to Yong and enfeoffed Bai at the old capital, Pingyang.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> names Duke Wu as the ruler who began the practice of funerary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. A later ruler, Duke Mu, who died in 621 BCE, had 177 people buried with him, among them several senior officials. The practice lasted for almost three centuries until Duke Xian, also called Shixi, banned it in 384 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duke Wu of Qin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Early Qin was ruled by dukes of the Ying clan who performed Zhou-style rituals while building a more centralised state than many of their neighbours; the Shiji records that <b>Duke Wu of Qin</b> created counties (xian) in conquered territory in 688 BCE, among the earliest known in China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
